<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Love These Entertainers – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love These Entertainers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for a simple game or craft. Interactive: Write simple comments on a peer's short text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write clear instructions for a game about identifying famous Panamanians and their achievements, including opening and closing steps (e.g., "Step 1: Each player draws a card. Step 2: Give clues about the figure's achievements. Step 3: Reveal the correct answer.")
Can write brief, supportive comments on a peer's text about a famous Panamanian (e.g., "Roberto Duran is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world." "I enjoyed reading about Roberto Duran.")
Can provide feedback on a classmate's description of a notable figure (e.g., "I didn't know Mariano Rivera won so many awards.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of 3-4 famous Panamanians (e.g., Roberto Durán, Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon, Sech) on the board or screen. Students are asked to quickly write down the names of the individuals and one achievement for each, activating prior knowledge from previous lessons related to the theme "I Love These Entertainers."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple instruction for a mini-game about identifying famous Panamanians. For example, "Step 1: Write the name of a famous athlete on a card." The teacher explicitly shows how to form clear, concise sentences using imperative verbs, focusing on correct capitalization and punctuation for instructions. The teacher also models the pronunciation of -ing and -ed endings while reading example sentences containing verbs like "watching" or "performed."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main goal of writing instructions?" (Expected answer: "To tell people how to do something.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Should instructions be long or short and clear?" (Expected answer: "Short and clear.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Famous Panamanians".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents two short texts: (1) A set of simple instructions for a game called "Guess the Panamanian Celebrity" (genre: instructions, register: informal, format: bulleted list). (2) A brief written description of a famous Panamanian (e.g., Roberto Durán), followed by two short, supportive comments from peers (genre: short descriptive text + comments, register: informal, format: paragraph + short sentences).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Instructions:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Step 1: Each player writes three clues about a famous Panamanian entertainer on a card."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Step 2: Players take turns reading their clues aloud. The other players guess the name."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Step 3: The first player to guess correctly wins a point."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text & Comments:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Roberto Durán is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world. He won many awards during his career and inspired many sports fans. He performed in many competitions."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Comment 1: I enjoyed reading about Roberto Durán. He is truly a famous athlete!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Comment 2: I didn't know he won so many awards. Great text!"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Written expression and interaction (describing achievements, giving instructions, providing feedback).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match the example instructions to pictures showing the actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify True/False statements about the presented instructions or the text about Roberto Durán (e.g., "The game has only two steps." - False; "Roberto Durán is considered a singer." - False).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using present continuous for future events, comparatives/superlatives, and past simple for achievements. They will use vocabulary words such as <i>entertainer, athlete, achievement, award, singer, actor, recognition, competition, performance, sports fan, win, achieve, inspire, train, perform, compete, famous, popular, talented, skilled, specialized, more, best.</i> Practice reading inflectional endings (-ed, -ing) in sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Cloze):</b> The teacher provides a paragraph about a famous Panamanian with blanks. Students fill in the blanks using the correct form of verbs (past simple for achievements) and adjectives (comparatives/superlatives) from a word bank. (e.g., "Atheyna Bylon ____ (win) a medal. She is ____ (skilled) than many other boxers.") Students then read their completed sentences aloud, focusing on the correct pronunciation of -ed endings (e.g., "won," "inspired," "trained").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Building):</b> Students are given sentence starters and prompts to write short sentences describing achievements or future plans of famous Panamanians. (e.g., "Mariano Rivera / win / many awards. -> Mariano Rivera won many awards." "Sech / perform / a concert next month. -> Sech is performing a concert next month."). They must include at least one comparative or superlative sentence (e.g., "Sech is more popular than other singers."). Students focus on accurate sentence structure and embedding target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Practice - Instruction Sequencing):</b> In pairs, students receive scrambled steps for a simple game related to famous Panamanians. They must write them in the correct order, numbering each step, ensuring clear and concise instructions. (e.g., "Give clues about the figure's achievements." "Reveal the correct answer." "Each player draws a card.") They then orally present one ordered instruction sequence, practicing the clear articulation of each step.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Famous Panamanians".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative written product and peer feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1 (Productive Writing):</b> In small groups (3-4 students), learners brainstorm a simple game about identifying famous Panamanians and their achievements. They collaboratively write clear, step-by-step instructions for their game. The instructions must include opening and closing steps and use at least three grammar points (past simple, present continuous for future, comparatives/superlatives) and five target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>athlete, achievement, win, inspire, best, performing</i>). For example, "Step 1: Each player draws a card with a famous Panamanian's name. Step 2: Player A gives clues about the figure's achievements (e.g., 'He won an award for boxing.'). Player B is guessing. Step 3: If Player B guesses correctly, they get a point. Player A is starting the next round."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2 (Interactive Writing):</b> Groups exchange their game instructions with another group. Each student in the receiving group reads the instructions and writes one brief, supportive comment or feedback on a separate sticky note, focusing on clarity, completeness, and interesting facts about the Panamanians mentioned (e.g., "I enjoyed reading about your game! I didn't know Mariano Rivera won so many awards. Your instructions are clear."). Comments must use appropriate language for feedback (e.g., "Good job," "I liked," "I learned").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a rubric for the written game instructions and peer comments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their group's written game instructions and their individual written peer comments/feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and logical sequence of instructions (Are the steps easy to follow?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target grammar (past simple, present continuous, comparatives/superlatives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and varied use of target vocabulary (entertainer, athlete, achievement, win, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in spelling, punctuation, and sentence structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Constructive and supportive nature of peer comments.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write short answers to the following questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or grammar point I used correctly in my writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I still need to practice when writing instructions or comments?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher and Student Reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about writing clear instructions for a game?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about famous Panamanians from my classmates' work or comments?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., student engagement in collaborative writing, correct application of past simple for achievements).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with writing clear instructions or applying specific grammar points (e.g., comparatives/superlatives)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better integrate pronunciation practice with writing tasks in future lessons? (e.g., encouraging students to read their work aloud to check for flow and naturalness).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students summarizing information about famous Panamanians and their achievements, potentially presenting their game instructions, and integrating the language learned across all four skills into a final project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>